{"study_accession":"PRJNA1123445","secondary_study_accession":"SRP513634","title":"Interferon gamma rebalances immunopathological signatures in Chronic Granulomatous Disease patients: a prototype for host directed therapy during a complex immune dysregulation","description":"Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) is a primary immunodeficiency characterized by recurrent life-threatening infections and hyperinflammatory complications. It is caused by mutations in the NADPH oxidase complex and the consequent loss of reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. Recombinant human interferon gamma (rIFN-gamma) is used as prophylaxis to reduce the risk of severe infections, but the mechanisms behind its efficacy in CGD are still unknown, as it does not restore NADPH oxidase-dependent ROS production. Here, we show that innate immune myeloid cells from CGD patients are epigenetically and functionally reprogrammed to have a hyperactivated immune status. In parallel, they present an impaired in vitro induction of trained immunity. CGD monocytes have deficient intracellular amino acids levels and have profound functional metabolic defects, both at the glycolytic and mitochondrial level. In vitro treatment with rIFN-gamma restored these myeloid metabolic defects and reduced abnormal IL-1beta and IL-6 production in response to fungal stimuli in CGD monocytes, suggesting that prophylactic rIFNgamma efficacy in CGD patients has a metabolic basis. These data shed light on the effects of ROS deficiency in the context of immunometabolism and pose the basis for targeting this immunometabolic axis, potentially beyond CGD, with rIFN-gamma immunotherapy.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":15,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN41811420","study_accession":"PRJNA1123445","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"Human sample from Homo sapiens","description":"Human sample from Homo sapiens","sex":"not provided","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_pub